Create one campaign-map background for Chapter 1 of Urkaaaz, "The Green
Frontier". Use the attached Green Frontier biome background as the
environment reference.

## Reference input

- Attach only the latest user-provided Green Frontier biome background. It
  replaces any earlier Green Frontier reference image for this generation.
- Preserve its bright rolling grasslands, warm golden dirt paths, rocky
  outcrops, evergreen stands, waterways, waterfalls, distant mountains,
  lighting, and overall color palette.
- Treat the image as an environment reference, not as a layout to copy or a
  requirement to reproduce gameplay terrain geometry. Design one clear
  campaign route; do not treat every existing path or crossing as a level
  location.
- Do not introduce visual features from Frostbound, Copperwood, Ashen March,
  Sunken Marshes, or another unrelated biome.

## Output

- Generate exactly one PNG:
  `campaign_chapter_01_green_frontier_map.png`.
- Save it under `assets_src/campaign/chapter-maps/`.
- Canvas: exactly 1920 x 1080 pixels, landscape aspect ratio 16:9.
- Fill the canvas edge-to-edge as a complete campaign map background.
- Do not add a frame, border, title plaque, or transparent margins.
- This is a background asset only. It will be displayed with its aspect ratio
  preserved; level markers and all screen UI are layered separately.

## Map composition

- Depict a painterly, top-down atlas view of the Green Frontier. Give the
  landscape enough terrain depth and readable environmental detail to feel
  like a place in the Urkaaaz world, while keeping the route and level sites
  clear at a small landscape-screen display size.
- Show exactly ten distinct, visually identifiable, empty level locations.
  Distribute them across the map with generous separation so ten independent
  marker overlays and touch targets can be placed later.
- Make each location easy to identify as a quiet anchor area, such as a
  clearing, a bend in the route, or a natural terrain feature. Keep the area
  behind each future marker visually simple and high-contrast enough for
  separate artwork and status treatments.
- Connect the ten locations with a coherent, readable travel route that
  suggests chapter progression. The route may branch or wind naturally, but
  it must not make the order of the ten sites ambiguous.
- Keep all ten locations comfortably inside the canvas, away from the extreme
  edges. Leave breathing room for screen controls that will be layered above
  the image; do not put a level location in a corner or directly against an
  edge.
- Compose an attractive, balanced map rather than a flat diagram. The ten
  level locations must remain the clearest repeated points of interest.

## Landmark and UI separation

- Do not draw fortresses, castles, keeps, boss characters, boss buildings,
  towers, ruins that read as a fortress, or other settlement silhouettes at
  any of the ten level locations. Fortress and boss illustrations are
  independent replaceable marker assets.
- Do not draw marker badges, marker frames, numbered pins, level icons,
  completion symbols, lock symbols, status rings, or placeholder circles.
- Do not include any text, letters, numbers, chapter or level labels, legends,
  UI, buttons, interface panels, or watermark.
- Do not bake level state, unlock state, or player progression into the art.
- Do not include a map title, compass rose, grid, route numbers, or decorative
  symbols that could be mistaken for level markers.

## Style and readability

- Match a polished, painterly fantasy atlas style suitable for the Urkaaaz
  campaign. Keep the environment cohesive with the attached Green Frontier
  reference rather than generic fantasy concept art.
- Use clear value separation between the travel route, the ten empty level
  locations, and surrounding terrain. Avoid excessive foliage, texture, fog,
  glow, or small detail that would obscure marker placement at device scale.
- Keep each empty location distinguishable from ordinary scenery without
  turning it into a pre-drawn icon or structure.
- Do not add characters, armies, combat, projectiles, or gameplay scenes.

## Negative prompt

text, letters, numbers, labels, title, logo, watermark, signature, UI, buttons,
panels, map legend, compass rose, grid, route numbers, numbered pins, badges,
icons, marker circles, status rings, locks, completion marks, fortress,
castle, keep, boss, boss building, tower, settlement, fortress silhouette,
generic fantasy map, snow, ice, desert, marsh, swamp, ashland, unrelated
biome, tiny unreadable details, crowded route, overlapping locations, fewer
than ten locations, more than ten locations, locations at the canvas edge,
characters, army, combat, projectile, excessive fog, excessive glow

## Review checklist

- Confirm the image is exactly 1920 x 1080 and fills the 16:9 canvas.
- Count exactly ten distinct, empty level locations; check that each is
  separated enough for an independent marker and touch target.
- Confirm the route connects the locations in an understandable progression.
- Confirm no fortress or boss artwork, marker placeholders, text, numbers, or
  UI is baked into the background.
- Compare the terrain, vegetation, lighting, and palette with the attached
  Green Frontier biome reference.
- Preview at the intended landscape-screen size and verify all ten locations
  remain readable and have clear space for overlays.
- After approval, measure and record the ten anchor coordinates against this
  exact image. Do not estimate final coordinates from the prompt.
- Do not import the generated image into Android until it is reviewed and
  approved.
